BSD support: status and known issues

Status
------

- FreeBSD: builds and runs the shell test suite. CI boots a stock FreeBSD
  cloud image under qemu (.github/workflows/bsd.yaml, test/ci/bsd-vm-run.sh)
  and runs test/run-tests.py in the guest. Tests that need Linux-only
  features (fusermount, utab, cuse, setuid handling, capabilities, write
  cache) skip themselves on FreeBSD.
- NetBSD, DragonFlyBSD: compile-time #ifdefs exist, nothing is built or
  tested. NetBSD ships its own librefuse; libfuse on NetBSD is untried.
- The FreeBSD kernel never requests READDIRPLUS, so only the plain readdir
  path of a filesystem is exercised there.

Inode numbers and getcwd(3)
---------------------------

- On Linux the d_ino a filesystem returns from readdir is only handed to
  userspace; the kernel never compares it (getcwd(3) walks the dcache), so
  0 or -1 is harmless there.
- On FreeBSD fusefs copies it into d_fileno unchanged, and getcwd(3) and
  the kernel's __getcwd() find the name of a directory by scanning the
  parent for the entry whose d_fileno equals the st_ino returned by
  stat(2). All FUSE entries share one st_dev, so no other signal exists;
  every readdir entry must carry the st_ino of the same name, or getcwd
  fails with ENOENT for every directory.
- A passthrough filesystem with a mount point inside its source tree cannot
  keep them equal from the source numbers: getdents reports the covered
  directory's inode, stat the mounted root's. getcwd() then fails with
  ENOENT (https://github.com/libfuse/libfuse/issues/962).
- High-level API without use_ino: fuse_new() sets readdir_ino on FreeBSD
  and NetBSD, so getattr reports the libfuse nodeid and readdir reports
  the nodeid for names that have been looked up. Names never looked up get
  -1 (4294967295 in ls -i). getcwd works, since every path component has
  been looked up; ls -i on a fresh directory does not show usable numbers.
- Low-level API, or use_ino on the high-level API: the filesystem is
  responsible. readdir entries with d_type DT_DIR (or DT_UNKNOWN) need a
  stat to get the right number; regular files cannot be mount points. That
  stat crosses into a mount point and hits the deadlock below if the
  entry is the filesystem's own mount point. The passthrough examples do
  not do this yet.

Deadlock when the daemon stats its own mount point
--------------------------------------------------

- vop_stdvptocnp() keeps the parent vnode locked shared across VOP_READDIR,
  i.e. across the wait for the daemon's READDIR reply. If the daemon, while
  serving that readdir, stats an entry that is its own mount point, the
  lookup crosses the mount and takes the FUSE root exclusive, which waits on
  the lock the readdir caller holds. Both sides sleep until daemon_timeout
  (60 s) or a signal.
- Hit by example/passthrough --plus mounted anywhere below /, since it
  mirrors /. Plain readdir does not stat and is not affected.
- Avoid it by mounting outside the mirrored tree:
  -o modules=subdir,subdir=DIR restricts the passthrough to DIR.
- -o intr lets a signal break the wait instead of the timeout.
- A fusefs fix (MNTK_LOOKUP_SHARED or an own vop_vptocnp) is the real
  solution; not reported upstream yet.

Console warnings
----------------

- "FUSE protocol violation ... file has different inode number and nodeid"
  is printed by fusefs whenever st_ino differs from the nodeid, e.g. with
  use_ino on the high-level API. Warning only.
